Chapter: CellGraph
  cites Sheets chapter CellFormula
  cites Sheets chapter CellStore
  cites Works chapter GopAhci
  cites Foreword chapter CCE

 Recalculation order, DERIVED. A formula's dependencies are the cells its
 expression names, an edge runs from a named cell to the cell naming it,
 and the order is a topological sort of that graph. Row-major order and
 the order the formulas were typed in are both wrong and both look right
 on a sheet small enough to read, which is why the order is computed and
 never assumed.

 A cycle is an ERROR NAMING THE CELLS, never a spin and never a fuel cap
 that answers a number. Kahn's algorithm leaves exactly the cycle members
 unemitted, so the entries the sort could not place ARE the report.

 A RANGE IS EXPANDED HERE and not in the parser. `formula-refs` answers a
 range's two corners because the parser does not know the sheet's bounds;
 this chapter holds the sheet, so `A1:B9` becomes an edge from every cell
 in the rectangle that carries a formula of its own.

 Formulas live BESIDE the cells rather than inside them. A cell in
 CellStore is a kind and a four-byte payload, and a parsed expression
 does not fit in four bytes; a formula table keyed by cell also keeps the
 store's dense block a fixed size per sheet.

 The adjacency is CSR over dense blocks, counted then filled, so the sort
 is one pass over vertices plus one over edges rather than a scan of
 every edge per vertex. The chain SHEET-7 measures is 1,000 vertices and
 999 edges, where the difference is a million comparisons against two
 thousand.

 We say:

Section: The Formula Table

  FCell = record {
    fc-row : Integer,
    fc-col : Integer,
    fc-src : Text,
    fc-expr : Expr,
    fc-ok : Boolean,
    fc-msg : Text
  }

  Program = record {
    pg-blk : Integer,
    pg-cells : List FCell,
    pg-slot : Integer
  }

  Plan = record {
    pl-ok : Boolean,
    pl-order : List Integer,
    pl-stuck : List Integer,
    pl-msg : Text
  }

  gr-word : Integer = 4

  graph-new : Integer -> Program
  graph-new (blk) =
    let n = sheet-rows blk * sheet-cols blk
    in Program { pg-blk = blk, pg-cells = [], pg-slot = alloc-zeroed (n * gr-word) 64 }

  gr-index : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gr-index (blk) (r) (c) = r * sheet-cols blk + c

  gr-slot-at : Program, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gr-slot-at (pg) (r) (c) =
    if sheet-in (pg.pg-blk) r c == False then 0
    else peek-32 (pg.pg-slot) (gr-index (pg.pg-blk) r c * gr-word)

 A second formula written to the same cell REPLACES the first entry
 rather than appending a second, because two entries for one cell would
 both be sorted and both evaluated, and the later one would win by
 accident of order.

  graph-put : Program, Integer, Integer, Text -> Program
  graph-put (pg) (r) (c) (src) =
    if sheet-in (pg.pg-blk) r c == False then pg
    else gr-put-parsed pg r c src (formula-parse src)

 A PROGRAM IS A WORKBOOK, NOT A VALUE, and `graph-put` says so by
 extending it IN PLACE and answering the same workbook. An earlier
 binding names the same workbook and sees the new formula.

 The contract is stated rather than avoided because avoiding it costs a
 quadratic: growing the table by concatenation allocates a fresh list of
 length 1, 2, 3 and so on, which is 17 MB at a thousand cells and 272 MB
 at four thousand, on a heap with no collector. `pg-slot` was already
 shared this way, being a block, so a Program was never a value a caller
 could hold two versions of; the cell list was the half that pretended
 otherwise.

  gr-put-parsed : Program, Integer, Integer, Text, FxParse -> Program
  gr-put-parsed (pg) (r) (c) (src) (p) =
    let e = FCell { fc-row = r, fc-col = c, fc-src = src, fc-expr = p.px-expr, fc-ok = p.px-ok, fc-msg = p.px-msg }
    in let old = gr-slot-at pg r c
    in if old > 0 then Program { pg-blk = pg.pg-blk, pg-cells = list-set-at (pg.pg-cells) (old - 1) e, pg-slot = pg.pg-slot }
    else gr-append pg e r c

  gr-append : Program, FCell, Integer, Integer -> Program
  gr-append (pg) (e) (r) (c) =
    let cells = list-push (pg.pg-cells) e
    in let s = poke-32 (pg.pg-slot) (gr-index (pg.pg-blk) r c * gr-word) (list-length cells)
    in Program { pg-blk = pg.pg-blk, pg-cells = cells, pg-slot = pg.pg-slot }

  graph-count : Program -> Integer
  graph-count (pg) = list-length (pg.pg-cells)

  graph-entry : Program, Integer -> FCell
  graph-entry (pg) (i) = list-at (pg.pg-cells) i

  graph-name : Program, Integer -> Text
  graph-name (pg) (i) =
    let e = list-at (pg.pg-cells) i
    in gr-col-name (e.fc-col) & show (e.fc-row + 1)

 A column name is base 26 with no zero digit, so the loop peels the low
 letter and divides by 26 after subtracting one, and A is 1 rather than
 0.

  gr-col-name : Integer -> Text
  gr-col-name (c) =
    if c < 26 then utf8-bytes-to-text [65 + c]
    else gr-col-name ((c / 26) - 1) & utf8-bytes-to-text [65 + (c - (c / 26) * 26)]

Section: The Edges

 An edge runs FROM a cell a formula names TO the cell holding that
 formula, because the named cell must carry a value before the naming
 one is evaluated. A reference to a cell with no formula of its own is a
 constant and contributes no edge.

 The edge list is built with `list-push`, which extends in place: the
 list is created inside `graph-edges` from a fresh `[]`, consumed by
 `graph-csr`, and handed to nobody. Concatenation here allocated a new
 list per edge and cost n squared over two cells, most of the 17 MB a
 thousand-cell chain took before this line changed.

  gr-edge : Program, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  gr-edge (pg) (r) (c) (to) (acc) =
    let s = gr-slot-at pg r c
    in if s == 0 then acc
    else list-push (list-push acc (s - 1)) to

  gr-lo : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gr-lo (a) (b) = if a < b then a else b

  gr-hi : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gr-hi (a) (b) = if a > b then a else b

 A range written backwards (`B9:A1`) names the same rectangle as the one
 written forwards, so the corners are ordered here rather than refused.

  gr-range-cols : Program, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  gr-range-cols (pg) (r) (c) (c2) (to) (acc) =
    if c > c2 then acc
    else gr-range-cols pg r (c + 1) c2 to (gr-edge pg r c to acc)

  gr-range-rows : Program, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  gr-range-rows (pg) (r) (r2) (c) (c2) (to) (acc) =
    if r > r2 then acc
    else gr-range-rows pg (r + 1) r2 c c2 to (gr-range-cols pg r c c2 to acc)

  gr-range : Program, RangeRef, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  gr-range (pg) (g) (to) (acc) =
    let a = g.rr-from
    in let b = g.rr-to
    in gr-range-rows pg (gr-lo (a.cr-row) (b.cr-row)) (gr-hi (a.cr-row) (b.cr-row)) (gr-lo (a.cr-col) (b.cr-col)) (gr-hi (a.cr-col) (b.cr-col)) to acc

  gr-walk-args : Program, List Expr, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  gr-walk-args (pg) (args) (i) (n) (to) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else gr-walk-args pg args (i + 1) n to (gr-walk pg (list-at args i) to acc)

  gr-walk : Program, Expr, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  gr-walk (pg) (e) (to) (acc) = when e
   is ExNum (v) -> acc
   is ExText (t) -> acc
   is ExRef (r) -> gr-edge pg (r.cr-row) (r.cr-col) to acc
   is ExRange (g) -> gr-range pg g to acc
   is ExNeg (x) -> gr-walk pg x to acc
   is ExBin (b) -> gr-walk pg (b.bn-right) to (gr-walk pg (b.bn-left) to acc)
   is ExCall (c) -> gr-walk-args pg (c.cn-args) 0 (list-length (c.cn-args)) to acc

  gr-edges-from : Program, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  gr-edges-from (pg) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else if (list-at (pg.pg-cells) i).fc-ok == False then gr-edges-from pg (i + 1) n acc
    else gr-edges-from pg (i + 1) n (gr-walk pg ((list-at (pg.pg-cells) i).fc-expr) i acc)

  graph-edges : Program -> List Integer
  graph-edges (pg) = gr-edges-from pg 0 (graph-count pg) []

Section: Compressed Adjacency

 Counted, prefix-summed, then filled: `gr-start` holds where each
 vertex's successors begin and `gr-adj` holds the successors themselves.

  Csr = record {
    cs-n : Integer,
    cs-e : Integer,
    cs-start : Integer,
    cs-adj : Integer,
    cs-indeg : Integer
  }

  gr-bump : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gr-bump (blk) (at) = poke-32 blk (at * gr-word) (peek-32 blk (at * gr-word) + 1)

  gr-count-pairs : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gr-count-pairs (es) (i) (n) (outc) (indeg) =
    if i >= n then 0
    else let a = gr-bump outc (list-at es i)
    in let b = gr-bump indeg (list-at es (i + 1))
    in gr-count-pairs es (i + 2) n outc indeg

  gr-prefix : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gr-prefix (outc) (start) (i) (n) (run) =
    if i > n then 0
    else let s = poke-32 start (i * gr-word) run
    in if i == n then 0
    else gr-prefix outc start (i + 1) n (run + peek-32 outc (i * gr-word))

  gr-fill-pairs : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gr-fill-pairs (es) (i) (n) (start) (cursor) (adj) =
    if i >= n then 0
    else let from = list-at es i
    in let at = peek-32 start (from * gr-word) + peek-32 cursor (from * gr-word)
    in let w = poke-32 adj (at * gr-word) (list-at es (i + 1))
    in let b = gr-bump cursor from
    in gr-fill-pairs es (i + 2) n start cursor adj

  graph-csr : Program -> Csr
  graph-csr (pg) =
    let n = graph-count pg
    in let es = graph-edges pg
    in let m = list-length es
    in let outc = alloc-zeroed (n * gr-word + gr-word) 64
    in let indeg = alloc-zeroed (n * gr-word + gr-word) 64
    in let c = gr-count-pairs es 0 m outc indeg
    in let start = alloc-zeroed (n * gr-word + gr-word + gr-word) 64
    in let p = gr-prefix outc start 0 n 0
    in let cursor = alloc-zeroed (n * gr-word + gr-word) 64
    in let adj = alloc-zeroed (m * gr-word + gr-word) 64
    in let f = gr-fill-pairs es 0 m start cursor adj
    in Csr { cs-n = n, cs-e = m / 2, cs-start = start, cs-adj = adj, cs-indeg = indeg }

Section: The Sort

 Kahn's algorithm over the CSR. The queue is a dense block used as a
 FIFO: every vertex enters at most once, so the block is exactly n slots
 and the head never passes the tail by more than the vertices emitted.

  gr-seed : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gr-seed (indeg) (q) (i) (n) (tail) =
    if i >= n then tail
    else if peek-32 indeg (i * gr-word) /= 0 then gr-seed indeg q (i + 1) n tail
    else let w = poke-32 q (tail * gr-word) i
    in gr-seed indeg q (i + 1) n (tail + 1)

  gr-relax : Csr, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gr-relax (g) (q) (v) (k) (e) (tail) =
    if k >= e then tail
    else let to = peek-32 (g.cs-adj) (k * gr-word)
    in let d = poke-32 (g.cs-indeg) (to * gr-word) (peek-32 (g.cs-indeg) (to * gr-word) - 1)
    in if peek-32 (g.cs-indeg) (to * gr-word) /= 0 then gr-relax g q v (k + 1) e tail
    else let w = poke-32 q (tail * gr-word) to
    in gr-relax g q v (k + 1) e (tail + 1)

  gr-drain : Csr, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  gr-drain (g) (q) (head) (tail) (acc) =
    if head >= tail then acc
    else let v = peek-32 q (head * gr-word)
    in let e = peek-32 (g.cs-start) (v * gr-word + gr-word)
    in let t2 = gr-relax g q v (peek-32 (g.cs-start) (v * gr-word)) e tail
    in gr-drain g q (head + 1) t2 (list-push acc v)

  gr-stuck : Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  gr-stuck (indeg) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else if peek-32 indeg (i * gr-word) == 0 then gr-stuck indeg (i + 1) n acc
    else gr-stuck indeg (i + 1) n (list-push acc i)

  gr-names : Program, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  gr-names (pg) (xs) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else if i == 0 then gr-names pg xs (i + 1) n (graph-name pg (list-at xs i))
    else gr-names pg xs (i + 1) n (acc & " " & graph-name pg (list-at xs i))

 A formula that did not parse is reported before the sort, because an
 order computed over a tree that does not exist is an answer to a
 question nobody asked.

  gr-first-bad : Program, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gr-first-bad (pg) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then 0 - 1
    else if (list-at (pg.pg-cells) i).fc-ok == False then i
    else gr-first-bad pg (i + 1) n

  graph-plan : Program -> Plan
  graph-plan (pg) =
    let bad = gr-first-bad pg 0 (graph-count pg)
    in if bad >= 0 then gr-plan-bad pg bad
    else gr-plan-sort pg (graph-csr pg)

  gr-plan-bad : Program, Integer -> Plan
  gr-plan-bad (pg) (bad) =
    Plan { pl-ok = False, pl-order = [], pl-stuck = [bad], pl-msg = graph-name pg bad & " does not parse: " & (list-at (pg.pg-cells) bad).fc-msg }

  gr-plan-sort : Program, Csr -> Plan
  gr-plan-sort (pg) (g) =
    let q = alloc-zeroed ((g.cs-n) * gr-word + gr-word) 64
    in let tail = gr-seed (g.cs-indeg) q 0 (g.cs-n) 0
    in let order = gr-drain g q 0 tail []
    in if list-length order == g.cs-n then Plan { pl-ok = True, pl-order = order, pl-stuck = [], pl-msg = "" }
    else gr-plan-cycle pg g order

  gr-plan-cycle : Program, Csr, List Integer -> Plan
  gr-plan-cycle (pg) (g) (order) =
    let stuck = gr-stuck (g.cs-indeg) 0 (g.cs-n) []
    in Plan { pl-ok = False, pl-order = order, pl-stuck = stuck, pl-msg = "a cycle through " & gr-names pg stuck 0 (list-length stuck) "" }

  graph-order-names : Program, Plan -> Text
  graph-order-names (pg) (p) = gr-names pg (p.pl-order) 0 (list-length (p.pl-order)) "(none)"

Section: What An Edit Recomputes

 The transitive dependents of the edited cell, IN THE DERIVED ORDER.
 Reachability is marked in a dense block and the plan is then filtered by
 the mark, so the answer is a subsequence of the full order rather than a
 set a caller has to sort again. The edited cell is included, because the
 cell itself is recomputed first.

  gr-mark-succ : Csr, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gr-mark-succ (g) (mark) (q) (k) (e) (tail) =
    if k >= e then tail
    else let to = peek-32 (g.cs-adj) (k * gr-word)
    in if peek-32 mark (to * gr-word) /= 0 then gr-mark-succ g mark q (k + 1) e tail
    else let m = poke-32 mark (to * gr-word) 1
    in let w = poke-32 q (tail * gr-word) to
    in gr-mark-succ g mark q (k + 1) e (tail + 1)

  gr-reach : Csr, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gr-reach (g) (mark) (q) (head) (tail) =
    if head >= tail then 0
    else let v = peek-32 q (head * gr-word)
    in let e = peek-32 (g.cs-start) (v * gr-word + gr-word)
    in let t2 = gr-mark-succ g mark q (peek-32 (g.cs-start) (v * gr-word)) e tail
    in gr-reach g mark q (head + 1) t2

  gr-filter : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  gr-filter (order) (i) (n) (mark) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else if peek-32 mark (list-at order i * gr-word) == 0 then gr-filter order (i + 1) n mark acc
    else gr-filter order (i + 1) n mark (list-push acc (list-at order i))

 The sort consumes `cs-indeg`, so the dependents walk builds its own CSR
 rather than reusing the sorted one. Two builds of a graph this size cost
 less than a subtle sharing fault.

 THE EDITED CELL USUALLY HOLDS NO FORMULA. A data cell is a constant and
 therefore has no vertex, so the roots of the walk are the formulas that
 name the cell DIRECTLY, found by asking each expression whether it uses
 the cell. A cell that does hold a formula is its own single root.

  gr-in-range : RangeRef, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  gr-in-range (g) (r) (c) =
    let a = g.rr-from
    in let b = g.rr-to
    in r >= gr-lo (a.cr-row) (b.cr-row) & r <= gr-hi (a.cr-row) (b.cr-row) & c >= gr-lo (a.cr-col) (b.cr-col) & c <= gr-hi (a.cr-col) (b.cr-col)

  gr-uses-args : List Expr, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  gr-uses-args (args) (i) (n) (r) (c) =
    if i >= n then False
    else if gr-uses (list-at args i) r c then True
    else gr-uses-args args (i + 1) n r c

  gr-uses : Expr, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  gr-uses (e) (r) (c) = when e
   is ExNum (v) -> False
   is ExText (t) -> False
   is ExRef (q) -> (q.cr-row) == r & (q.cr-col) == c
   is ExRange (g) -> gr-in-range g r c
   is ExNeg (x) -> gr-uses x r c
   is ExBin (b) -> gr-uses (b.bn-left) r c | gr-uses (b.bn-right) r c
   is ExCall (k) -> gr-uses-args (k.cn-args) 0 (list-length (k.cn-args)) r c

  gr-roots-scan : Program, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  gr-roots-scan (pg) (i) (n) (r) (c) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else if gr-uses ((list-at (pg.pg-cells) i).fc-expr) r c then gr-roots-scan pg (i + 1) n r c (list-push acc i)
    else gr-roots-scan pg (i + 1) n r c acc

 Every accumulator in this chapter is built with `list-push` and every
 one of them is created inside its own call from a fresh `[]`: the sort's
 order, the cycle report, the dependents filter and the edge list are
 each handed to one caller and dropped. That is the condition L-ALIAS
 names, and it is checked per accumulator rather than assumed.

  gr-roots : Program, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  gr-roots (pg) (r) (c) =
    let s = gr-slot-at pg r c
    in if s > 0 then [s - 1]
    else gr-roots-scan pg 0 (graph-count pg) r c []

  gr-seed-roots : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gr-seed-roots (rs) (i) (n) (mark) (q) (tail) =
    if i >= n then tail
    else if peek-32 mark (list-at rs i * gr-word) /= 0 then gr-seed-roots rs (i + 1) n mark q tail
    else let m = poke-32 mark (list-at rs i * gr-word) 1
    in let w = poke-32 q (tail * gr-word) (list-at rs i)
    in gr-seed-roots rs (i + 1) n mark q (tail + 1)

  graph-dependents : Program, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  graph-dependents (pg) (r) (c) =
    let plan = graph-plan pg
    in if plan.pl-ok == False then []
    else gr-deps-walk pg (graph-csr pg) (gr-roots pg r c) (plan.pl-order)

  gr-deps-walk : Program, Csr, List Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  gr-deps-walk (pg) (g) (rs) (order) =
    let mark = alloc-zeroed ((g.cs-n) * gr-word + gr-word) 64
    in let q = alloc-zeroed ((g.cs-n) * gr-word + gr-word) 64
    in let tail = gr-seed-roots rs 0 (list-length rs) mark q 0
    in let d = gr-reach g mark q 0 tail
    in gr-filter order 0 (list-length order) mark []

  graph-dependent-names : Program, Integer, Integer -> Text
  graph-dependent-names (pg) (r) (c) =
    let xs = graph-dependents pg r c
    in gr-names pg xs 0 (list-length xs) "(none)"

Page 1
